  "title": "Variance banding: how many runs before a benchmark number means anything",
  "question": "For each program benchmark, run n repetitions at one SHA, compute the variance band, and derive the smallest n at which a claimed improvement is distinguishable from noise. Without this, every regression and improvement claim in the program is uncalibrated.",
  "answer": "At SHA a923b5efa845\u2026, n=21 timed repetitions (warmup=3) per program surface. Per-benchmark mean, sample stdev, 95% CI of the mean, two-sample MDE (alpha=0.05, power=0.8), and n_min for a 5% claimed improvement are attached with the raw ns vector. Empty input is refused. Formula: two-sample MDE=(z_{1-alpha/2}+z_{power})*s*sqrt(2/n); CI95(mean)=mean +/- z_{1-alpha/2}*s/sqrt(n); n_min=ceil(2*((z_a+z_b)*s/delta)^2) for delta=0.05*|mean|; sample stdev with n-1; alpha=0.05 power=0.80; derived_from=raw.",
  "spec_anchor": "command-center/WORK-UNIT-CATALOG.json:TST-0109",
